<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>ilifu SLURM Training Cluster</title>
    <script src="https://cdn.tailwindcss.com"></script>
    <script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/alpinejs@3.x.x/dist/cdn.min.js"></script>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/highlight.js/11.11.1/styles/atom-one-dark.min.css">
    <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/highlight.js/11.11.1/highlight.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body class="bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-300 to-blue-500 min-h-screen flex items-center justify-center p-4">
    <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-2xl max-w-6xl w-full p-8">
        <div class="text-center mb-8">
            <img src="/static/ilifu-dark-blue-with-light-blue-dots.svg" alt="ilifu logo" class="h-16 mx-auto mb-4">
            <h1 class="text-5xl font-bold text-blue-600 mb-4">SLURM Training Cluster</h1>
            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">High-Performance Computing Environment for Learning</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Tab Navigation -->
        <div x-data="{ activeTab: 'access', workflowTab: 'nextflow' }" class="w-full">
            <!-- Tab Buttons -->
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-8 border-b-2 border-gray-200 pb-4 justify-center">
                <button @click="activeTab = 'overview'"
                    :class="activeTab === 'overview' ? 'bg-blue-600 text-white' : 'bg-gray-200 text-gray-800 hover:bg-gray-300'"
                    class="px-4 py-2 rounded font-semibold transition-colors">
                    Overview
                </button>
                <button @click="activeTab = 'access'"
                    :class="activeTab === 'access' ? 'bg-blue-600 text-white' : 'bg-gray-200 text-gray-800 hover:bg-gray-300'"
                    class="px-4 py-2 rounded font-semibold transition-colors">
                    Access the Cluster
                </button>
                <button @click="activeTab = 'slurm'"
                    :class="activeTab === 'slurm' ? 'bg-blue-600 text-white' : 'bg-gray-200 text-gray-800 hover:bg-gray-300'"
                    class="px-4 py-2 rounded font-semibold transition-colors">
                    SLURM Job Management
                </button>
                <button @click="activeTab = 'workflows'"
                    :class="activeTab === 'workflows' ? 'bg-blue-600 text-white' : 'bg-gray-200 text-gray-800 hover:bg-gray-300'"
                    class="px-4 py-2 rounded font-semibold transition-colors">
                    Workflows
                </button>
                <button @click="activeTab = 'containers'"
                    :class="activeTab === 'containers' ? 'bg-blue-600 text-white' : 'bg-gray-200 text-gray-800 hover:bg-gray-300'"
                    class="px-4 py-2 rounded font-semibold transition-colors">
                    Containers
                </button>
                <button @click="activeTab = 'other'"
                    :class="activeTab === 'other' ? 'bg-blue-600 text-white' : 'bg-gray-200 text-gray-800 hover:bg-gray-300'"
                    class="px-4 py-2 rounded font-semibold transition-colors">
                    Other
                </button>
            </div>

            <!-- Overview Tab -->
            <div x-show="activeTab === 'overview'" class="space-y-6">
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Cluster Features</h2>
                <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-2">📊 SLURM Scheduler</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">Multi-node job scheduling and resource management for distributed computing tasks</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-2">🐍 Interactive Computing</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">Jupyter notebooks for interactive data analysis and scientific computing</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-2">💾 Shared Storage</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">CephFS distributed storage for users, software, and data</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-2">🔐 User Management</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">LDAP-based authentication and centralized user management</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-2">🖥️ Compute Nodes</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">Multiple compute nodes with substantial memory for parallel processing</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-2">📚 Training Ready</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">Pre-configured environment designed for HPC training and education</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Access the Cluster Tab -->
            <div x-show="activeTab === 'access'" class="space-y-6">
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Access the Cluster</h2>

                <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-6">
                    <!-- SSH Access -->
                    <div class="bg-blue-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">🔒 SSH Access</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">Connect to the login node using SSH:</p>
                        <pre class="bg-gray-900 text-green-400 p-3 rounded text-sm mb-4 overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code>ssh username@{{ caddy.domain }}</code></pre>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">Replace <code class="bg-gray-200 px-1 rounded">username</code> with your login credentials</p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Jupyter Access -->
                    <div class="bg-blue-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">🐍 JupyterHub Access</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">Access interactive notebooks through the web:</p>
                        <a href="/jupyter/" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 text-white px-6 py-2 rounded font-semibold hover:bg-blue-700 transition-colors">
                            Launch JupyterHub
                        </a>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mt-4">Same credentials as SSH login</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- SSH Key Setup -->
                <div class="bg-amber-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-amber-600">
                    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-amber-700 mb-4">🔑 Setting Up SSH Keys (Recommended)</h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">For passwordless, secure access, generate an ed25519 SSH key pair:</p>

                    <div class="bg-gray-900 text-green-400 p-4 rounded mb-4">
                        <p class="text-sm mb-2 font-semibold">On your local machine:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash">ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "your-email@example.com"</code></pre>
                    </div>

                    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-2 text-sm"><strong>Steps:</strong></p>
                    <ol class="text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-1 ml-4 list-decimal">
                        <li>Press Enter to accept the default key location</li>
                        <li>Enter a secure passphrase (or press Enter for no passphrase)</li>
                        <li>Your public key is now at <code class="bg-gray-200 px-1 rounded">~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub</code></li>
                    </ol>

                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-4 mb-2 text-sm"><strong>Add your public key to the cluster:</strong></p>
                    <div class="bg-gray-900 text-green-400 p-4 rounded text-sm overflow-x-auto">
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash">ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub username@{{ caddy.domain }}</code></pre>
                    </div>

                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-4 text-sm">After this, you can SSH without entering a password!</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- SLURM Job Management Tab -->
            <div x-show="activeTab === 'slurm'" class="space-y-6">
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">SLURM Job Management</h2>

                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <!-- Job Submission -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">📝 Submitting Jobs</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Submit batch jobs using <code class="bg-gray-200 px-1 rounded">sbatch</code> or run interactive jobs with <code class="bg-gray-200 px-1 rounded">srun</code>:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
#SBATCH --job-name=my_job
#SBATCH --ntasks=4
#SBATCH --cpus-per-task=2
#SBATCH --mem=16G
#SBATCH --time=01:00:00
#SBATCH --partition=training

# Your commands here
echo "Running on $(hostname)"
sleep 30</code></pre>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4">Submit with: <code class="bg-gray-200 px-1 rounded">sbatch script.sh</code></p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Job Monitoring -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">👀 Monitoring Jobs</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Check job status and cluster information:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash"># View your running jobs
squeue -u $USER

# Check detailed job info
scontrol show job &lt;job_id&gt;

# View cluster node status
sinfo

# View account information
sacctmgr show accounts</code></pre>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Job Control -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">⚙️ Job Control</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Manage your running and completed jobs:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash"># Cancel a job
scancel &lt;job_id&gt;

# Cancel all your jobs
scancel -u $USER

# View job history
sacct --starttime=2025-01-01

# Get job details
sacct -j &lt;job_id&gt; --format=jobid,elapsed,state,exitcode</code></pre>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Partition Info -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">🎯 Partitions</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">The cluster has different partitions optimized for different workloads. View available partitions with:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash">sinfo
sinfo -p training  # View specific partition details</code></pre>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4">Specify partition in your job script: <code class="bg-gray-200 px-1 rounded">#SBATCH --partition=training</code></p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- SLURM Job Dependencies -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">⛓️ Job Dependencies</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Chain jobs together so they run in sequence. Useful for pipelines before you move to Cromwell/NextFlow:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash"># Submit first job and capture its ID
JOB1=$(sbatch --job-name=step1 step1.sh | awk '{print $4}')

# Submit dependent jobs
JOB2=$(sbatch --job-name=step2 --dependency=afterok:$JOB1 step2.sh | awk '{print $4}')
JOB3=$(sbatch --job-name=step3 --dependency=afterok:$JOB2 step3.sh | awk '{print $4}')

# Alternative dependency types:
# afterok:jobid  - Run after job succeeds
# afternotok:jobid - Run after job fails
# afterany:jobid - Run regardless of job outcome</code></pre>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4">Note: For complex workflows, consider using NextFlow or Cromwell instead of manual job dependencies.</p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Job Arrays -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">🔁 Job Arrays</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Run the same job many times with different parameters using job arrays:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
#SBATCH --job-name=array_job
#SBATCH --array=1-100
#SBATCH --cpus-per-task=2
#SBATCH --mem=8G
#SBATCH --time=01:00:00

# SLURM_ARRAY_TASK_ID contains the array index
echo "Processing sample $SLURM_ARRAY_TASK_ID"
python analysis.py --input data_${SLURM_ARRAY_TASK_ID}.txt --output results_${SLURM_ARRAY_TASK_ID}.txt</code></pre>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4">Submit with: <code class="bg-gray-200 px-1 rounded">sbatch array_job.sh</code> - This creates 100 jobs automatically!</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Workflows Tab -->
            <div x-show="activeTab === 'workflows'" class="space-y-6">
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Workflow Languages</h2>

                <!-- Workflows Introduction -->
                <div class="bg-blue-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600 mb-6">
                    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">What are Workflow Languages?</h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Workflow languages provide abstractions for building complex multi-step computational pipelines. They go beyond simple SLURM job submission by offering:</p>
                    <ul class="text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-1 ml-4 list-disc mb-4">
                        <li><strong>Task Composition:</strong> Chain multiple computational steps together</li>
                        <li><strong>Dependency Management:</strong> Automatically handle job dependencies and data flow</li>
                        <li><strong>Scalability:</strong> Run on single machine or large clusters transparently</li>
                        <li><strong>Reproducibility:</strong> Define entire pipelines in code for version control</li>
                        <li><strong>Resource Optimization:</strong> Efficiently allocate compute resources across tasks</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm"><strong>Two Popular Options:</strong> NextFlow (Groovy-based, data-centric) and WDL (XML-based, task-centric). Choose based on your workflow style and community needs.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- Workflow Sub-tabs -->
                <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-6 pb-2 border-b-2 border-gray-200">
                    <button @click="workflowTab = 'nextflow'"
                        :class="workflowTab === 'nextflow' ? 'bg-blue-600 text-white' : 'bg-gray-200 text-gray-800 hover:bg-gray-300'"
                        class="px-4 py-2 rounded font-semibold transition-colors text-sm">
                        NextFlow
                    </button>
                    <button @click="workflowTab = 'wdl'"
                        :class="workflowTab === 'wdl' ? 'bg-blue-600 text-white' : 'bg-gray-200 text-gray-800 hover:bg-gray-300'"
                        class="px-4 py-2 rounded font-semibold transition-colors text-sm">
                        WDL
                    </button>
                </div>

                <!-- NextFlow Sub-tab -->
                <div x-show="workflowTab === 'nextflow'" class="space-y-6">
                <div class="bg-blue-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600 mb-6">
                    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">What is NextFlow?</h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">NextFlow is a workflow orchestration platform built on Groovy that simplifies writing and executing data-intensive computational pipelines. It's particularly powerful for managing complex workflows across multiple data files and processes.</p>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4"><strong>Getting Started:</strong> First load the NextFlow module, then use the <code class="bg-gray-200 px-1 rounded">nx-ilifu</code> wrapper which automatically loads the cluster's centralized configuration:</p>
                    <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash">module add nextflow
nx-ilifu run workflow.nf -profile main_small</code></pre>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4"><strong>Available Profiles:</strong></p>
                    <ul class="text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-1 ml-4 list-disc">
                        <li><strong>standard</strong> - Local execution (good for testing)</li>
                        <li><strong>main_small</strong> - SLURM: 1 CPU, 4GB RAM (quick jobs)</li>
                        <li><strong>main_medium</strong> - SLURM: 8 CPUs, 64GB RAM (typical jobs)</li>
                        <li><strong>main_large</strong> - SLURM: 32 CPUs, 232GB RAM (large/parallel jobs)</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4">All SLURM profiles use the training partition and have Singularity enabled for containerized workflows.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <!-- Basic Workflow -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">📋 Basic Workflow Structure</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">A simple NextFlow workflow for data processing:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-groovy">#!/usr/bin/env nextflow

params.input = "data/*.txt"
params.outdir = "results"

process PROCESS_DATA {
    input:
    path data_file

    output:
    path "*.processed"

    script:
    """
    cat $data_file | wc -l > ${data_file.baseName}.processed
    """
}

workflow {
    data_files = Channel.fromPath(params.input)
    PROCESS_DATA(data_files)
}</code></pre>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Workflow-Specific Configuration -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">⚙️ Workflow-Specific Configuration</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">The cluster configuration (SLURM, Singularity, profiles) is automatically loaded by the <code class="bg-gray-200 px-1 rounded">nx-ilifu</code> wrapper. For workflow-specific settings, create a <code class="bg-gray-200 px-1 rounded">nextflow.config</code> in your project directory:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-groovy">// Project-specific parameters
params {
    projectName = "my_project"
    input = "data/*.fq"
    outDir = "results"
}

// Override specific process settings if needed
process {
    withName: 'HEAVY_PROCESS' {
        cpus = 16
        memory = 128.GB
    }
}</code></pre>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4">The cluster profiles (standard, main_small, main_medium, main_large) are always available and will be merged with your local config. Just use <code class="bg-gray-200 px-1 rounded">-profile main_medium</code> when running your workflow.</p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Running Workflows -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">🚀 Running Workflows</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Execute NextFlow workflows with SLURM:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash"># Run with default parameters
nextflow run workflow.nf

# Run with custom parameters
nextflow run workflow.nf --input "mydata/*.fq" --outdir "my_results"

# Resume a failed workflow
nextflow run workflow.nf -resume

# Run with specific profile
nextflow run workflow.nf -profile slurm

# Use SLURM resources
nextflow run workflow.nf -profile slurm --queue training --memory 16g</code></pre>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Multiple Processes -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">🔗 Chaining Processes</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Create pipelines by connecting multiple processes:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-groovy">process ALIGN {
    input:
    path reads

    output:
    path "*.bam"

    script:
    """
    bwa mem reference.fa $reads | samtools view -b > aligned.bam
    """
}

process SORT {
    input:
    path bam_file

    output:
    path "*.sorted.bam"

    script:
    """
    samtools sort $bam_file -o ${bam_file.baseName}.sorted.bam
    """
}

workflow {
    reads = Channel.fromPath("reads/*.fq")
    bams = ALIGN(reads)
    SORT(bams)
}</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                </div>

                <!-- WDL Sub-tab -->
                <div x-show="workflowTab === 'wdl'" class="space-y-6">
                <div class="bg-blue-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600 mb-6">
                    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">What is WDL?</h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">WDL (Workflow Description Language) is a task-centric language for defining computational workflows. Workflows written in WDL are executed by the Cromwell engine, which handles job scheduling, data management, and execution across different compute systems.</p>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4"><strong>Getting Started:</strong> Cromwell (the WDL executor) is available on the cluster. Load it with:</p>
                    <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash">module add cromwell</code></pre>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4">Key features: Task-based workflow definition, portable across backends, excellent for scientific workflows, built-in error handling and retry logic, works seamlessly with containers (Docker/Singularity).</p>
                </div>

                    <!-- Basic Task -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">📦 Defining Tasks</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">A WDL task defines a unit of work:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-wdl">task hello {
    input {
        String name
        String message = "Hello"
    }

    command {
        echo "~{message}, ~{name}!"
    }

    output {
        String result = read_string(stdout())
    }

    runtime {
        docker: "ubuntu:22.04"
        memory: "1 GB"
        cpu: 1
    }
}</code></pre>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Workflow Definition -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">🔧 Defining Workflows</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Workflows orchestrate tasks together:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-wdl">workflow hello_world {
    input {
        String person
    }

    call hello { input: name = person }

    output {
        String greeting = hello.result
    }
}

# Alternative: with multiple tasks
workflow data_pipeline {
    input {
        File input_file
    }

    call process_step1 { input: data = input_file }
    call process_step2 { input: data = process_step1.output }

    output {
        File final_result = process_step2.output
    }
}</code></pre>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Input JSON -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">📄 Input Files</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Create input JSON files (e.g., <code class="bg-gray-200 px-1 rounded">inputs.json</code>):</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-json">{
    "hello_world.person": "Alice",
    "hello_world.hello.message": "Hi"
}</code></pre>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4">Run with: <code class="bg-gray-200 px-1 rounded">cromwell run workflow.wdl -i inputs.json</code></p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Runtime Specification -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">⚙️ Runtime Configuration</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Configure compute resources for tasks:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-wdl">task compute_intensive {
    input {
        File data
    }

    command {
        python analysis.py ~{data}
    }

    output {
        File results = "results.txt"
    }

    runtime {
        docker: "python:3.9"
        memory: "32 GB"
        cpu: 8
        disks: "local-disk 100 HDD"
        time: 120  # in minutes
    }
}</code></pre>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Working with SLURM -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">🎯 Running on SLURM</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Use Cromwell backend configuration for SLURM:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-conf">include required(classpath("application"))

backend {
    default = slurm

    providers {
        slurm {
            actor-factory = "cromwell.backend.impl.sfs.config.ConfigBackendLifecycleActorFactory"
            config {
                runtime-attributes = """
                    Int cpu = 1
                    Int memory_mb = 1024
                    String queue = "training"
                    String walltime = "01:00:00"
                """

                submit = "sbatch --job-name=${job_name} --cpus-per-task=${cpu} --mem=${memory_mb}M --time=${walltime} --partition=${queue} ${script}"
                kill = "scancel ${job_id}"
                check-alive = "squeue -j ${job_id}"
                job-id-regex = "Submitted batch job (\\d+)"
            }
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Containers Tab -->
            <div x-show="activeTab === 'containers'" class="space-y-6">
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Container Technologies</h2>

                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <!-- Container Overview -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">📦 What are Containers?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Containers package your application and its dependencies into a self-contained unit that runs consistently across different environments. This is crucial for reproducible research and deployment in HPC.</p>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-2"><strong>Key Benefits:</strong></p>
                        <ul class="text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-1 ml-4 list-disc">
                            <li>Reproducibility: Same environment everywhere (local machine, cluster, cloud)</li>
                            <li>Portability: Run on any system with container runtime installed</li>
                            <li>Isolation: Dependencies don't conflict with system or other containers</li>
                            <li>Easy sharing: Distribute your entire analysis environment as a single image</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Docker Section -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">🐳 Docker</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Docker is the most popular containerization platform. Define environments using a Dockerfile:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash">FROM ubuntu:22.04

# Install dependencies
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y \
    python3 \
    python3-pip \
    build-essential

# Install Python packages
RUN pip install numpy scipy pandas scikit-learn

# Set working directory
WORKDIR /data

# Default command
CMD ["python3"]</code></pre>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 mt-4">Build and run:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash"># Build the image
docker build -t myanalysis:latest .

# Run a container
docker run -v /path/to/data:/data myanalysis:latest python3 analysis.py</code></pre>

                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4"><strong>Pros for HPC:</strong></p>
                        <ul class="text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-1 ml-4 list-disc">
                            <li>Easy to use and widely adopted in development</li>
                            <li>Large ecosystem with millions of pre-built images</li>
                            <li>Great for development, testing, and CI/CD pipelines</li>
                            <li>Excellent documentation and community support</li>
                        </ul>

                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4"><strong>Cons for HPC:</strong></p>
                        <ul class="text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-1 ml-4 list-disc">
                            <li><strong>Security:</strong> Requires root/sudo to run (major concern on shared clusters)</li>
                            <li><strong>Performance:</strong> Heavier weight and more overhead than Singularity</li>
                            <li><strong>HPC Integration:</strong> Not designed with HPC security model in mind</li>
                            <li><strong>Privilege Escalation:</strong> Root access requirement violates many HPC security policies</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Singularity Section -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">🚀 Singularity / Apptainer</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Singularity (now called Apptainer) was designed specifically for HPC. Define environments using a Definition File:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash">Bootstrap: docker
From: ubuntu:22.04

%post
    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y python3 python3-pip build-essential
    pip install numpy scipy pandas scikit-learn

%runscript
    python3 "$@"

%environment
    export PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
    export LC_ALL=C</code></pre>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 mt-4">Build and run:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash"># Build the image
singularity build myanalysis.sif myanalysis.def

# Run directly (no root required)
singularity run myanalysis.sif analysis.py input.txt

# Or use the image like an executable
./myanalysis.sif analysis.py input.txt

# Run with SLURM
sbatch --job-name=container_job \
  singularity exec myanalysis.sif python3 analysis.py</code></pre>

                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4"><strong>Pros for HPC:</strong></p>
                        <ul class="text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-1 ml-4 list-disc">
                            <li><strong>Security:</strong> Runs without root privileges (critical for shared HPC systems)</li>
                            <li><strong>Performance:</strong> Lightweight and minimal overhead</li>
                            <li><strong>HPC Native:</strong> Designed specifically for HPC environments and schedulers</li>
                            <li><strong>User Namespaces:</strong> Preserves user identity and permissions</li>
                            <li><strong>GPU/MPI:</strong> Native support for GPUs and MPI applications</li>
                            <li><strong>Scheduler Integration:</strong> Seamless integration with SLURM, PBS, etc.</li>
                        </ul>

                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4"><strong>Cons for HPC:</strong></p>
                        <ul class="text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-1 ml-4 list-disc">
                            <li>Smaller community and ecosystem compared to Docker</li>
                            <li>Less intuitive definition file syntax</li>
                            <li>Fewer pre-built community images available</li>
                            <li>Less common in general development workflows</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Practical Guidance -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">🎯 When to Use Which?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4"><strong>Use Docker for:</strong></p>
                        <ul class="text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-1 ml-4 list-disc">
                            <li>Local development and testing on your machine</li>
                            <li>CI/CD pipelines (GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, etc.)</li>
                            <li>Web applications and microservices</li>
                            <li>Quick prototyping and experimentation</li>
                        </ul>

                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4"><strong>Use Singularity for:</strong></p>
                        <ul class="text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-1 ml-4 list-disc">
                            <li>Production runs on HPC clusters (with SLURM or other schedulers)</li>
                            <li>Large-scale parallel jobs (MPI applications)</li>
                            <li>GPU-accelerated workloads on shared systems</li>
                            <li>Any production HPC environment</li>
                        </ul>

                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 mt-4"><strong>Converting Docker to Singularity:</strong></p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash"># You can build Singularity images directly from Docker images
singularity build myimage.sif docker://ubuntu:22.04

# Or from a Docker Hub image
singularity build myimage.sif docker://continuumio/anaconda3:latest

# Or convert from a Dockerfile by bootstrapping from Docker
# (use a Singularity definition file that references Docker)</code></pre>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Running on the Cluster -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">🖥️ Running Containers with SLURM</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Submit a container job to the cluster:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
#SBATCH --job-name=container_analysis
#SBATCH --cpus-per-task=4
#SBATCH --mem=8G
#SBATCH --time=01:00:00
#SBATCH --partition=training

# Load Singularity module (if needed)
module load singularity

# Run container with SLURM
singularity exec myanalysis.sif \
    python3 /data/analysis.py \
    --input input.txt \
    --output results.txt

# For GPU jobs
#SBATCH --gres=gpu:1
singularity exec --nv myanalysis.sif \
    python3 /data/gpu_analysis.py</code></pre>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4">Note: The <code class="bg-gray-200 px-1 rounded">--nv</code> flag enables GPU support in Singularity.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Other Tab -->
            <div x-show="activeTab === 'other'" class="space-y-6">
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Other Useful Tips & Tools</h2>

                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <!-- tmux Basics -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">📺 Terminal Multiplexing with tmux</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">tmux lets you manage multiple terminal sessions and windows in a single connection. This is invaluable for long-running jobs and managing multiple workflows:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash"># Create a new tmux session
tmux new-session -s mywork

# Attach to an existing session
tmux attach -t mywork

# Create a new window within session (Ctrl+b, then c)
# Switch between windows (Ctrl+b, then n or p)
# Create a split pane (Ctrl+b, then % for vertical or " for horizontal)
# Navigate panes (Ctrl+b, then arrow keys)

# List all sessions
tmux list-sessions

# Detach from session (Ctrl+b, then d)</code></pre>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4">Tip: Start a long-running job in tmux, detach, and reconnect later even if your SSH connection drops!</p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Version Control -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">🔀 Version Control with Git & GitHub</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Always use version control for your scripts and analysis code. This ensures reproducibility, enables collaboration, and lets you track changes:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash"># Initialize a Git repository
git init
git add *.py *.sh workflow.nf
git commit -m "Initial commit: analysis scripts and workflows"

# Create a README.md describing your project
# Then push to GitHub
git remote add origin https://github.com/username/myproject.git
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main

# Good practices:
# - Write meaningful commit messages
# - Create a README.md explaining your project
# - Use .gitignore for data files and outputs
# - Tag releases with meaningful versions (v1.0, v2.0, etc.)</code></pre>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mt-4">Benefits: Reproducibility, collaboration, change tracking, and a permanent record of your scientific work.</p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Resource Monitoring -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-4">📊 Resource Monitoring</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Monitor cluster resource usage to optimize your workflows:</p>
                        <pre class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code class="language-bash"># Real-time system monitoring
htop

# Quick CPU and memory stats
top -u $USER

# Check disk usage
df -h          # Overall filesystem usage
du -sh ~/.*    # Your directory usage

# Check SLURM queue
squeue

# Monitor a specific job
watch -n 5 'squeue -j &lt;job_id&gt;'</code></pre>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Pro Tips -->
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-600">
